                  <provenance type="#e"><orgName role="fmo" key="mlgbOrg_86">Bury St Edmunds,
                        Suffolk. Benedictine abbey of St Edmund, King and Martyr.
                        </orgName><p>'Liber monachorum sancti edmundi de empcione fratris henr' de
                        Kirk' in quo continentur subscripta videlicet. . .'</p><p>'S.97' (fols. 1r
                        and 2r).</p><p>Further notes by Henry de Kirkestede (as 'Ownership'
                        below).</p><q type="pressmark">S.97</q></provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">A note in Henry de Kirkestede's hand at the
                     top of fol. 13r reads 'Quaternus magistri W. elueden', prec' xl d.' A second
                     note by him at the foot of the same page reads 'In isto uolumine sunt 6.
                     quaternis'. So it would seem that fols. 1--76 (six quires) were acquired from
                     Walter de Elveden (d. by 1360), vicar-general of the bishop of Norwich (Emden,
                     BRUC 210--11). The likelihood is that the pages were acquired from his
                     executors.</provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">Purchased by Harley from James Anderson, 19
                     November 1725 (Diary of Humfrey Wanley, ed. Wright, 394).</provenance>
               </history>
